- 2026-09-13: v1.27.0 recurring envelope moves (bg session, E3 by context override, Nick: "Build it, and confirm it would be clear on the transaction row that it was a recurring move"). Execution is LAZY, not a worker: due runs materialise on the next grid / detail / transactions read and after the daily sync, the same way ensureMonth materialises the template, so there is no in-process scheduler to keep alive and a closed laptop just catches up on the next open. Each run is an ordinary money_movements row (actor "schedule", schedule_id, run_date) so the grid fold, running balances, History, the explainer and the transactions list see it with no second code path; consumers bucket by run_date so a caught-up run lands in the month it was due, not the month it was noticed. Idempotency is structural: a partial unique index on (user_id, schedule_id, run_date) plus next_run advancement inside one transaction. Overdraft policy: execute and show the deficit (matches the manual move's "allowed, but the zero-based alert will fire"); the earlier sketch's skip-and-flag was rejected because with lazy execution a skipped run has nobody to flag to and silently under-funds the destination. v1 restricts schedules to expense-family envelopes (no income sources: the income cap rule would need a partial-run policy). Cadence vocabulary reuses BudgetPeriod (weekly / fortnightly / monthly / annual) so the balance engine's boundary maths is the single source of stepping. Day-of-week and day-of-month are both expressed by the anchor date. Collision management: a peer bg session owns MoveMoneyModal / ISC-332..351 / v1.26.0, so this feature lives in a new RecurringMovesModal, takes ISC-352+, and ships as v1.27.0.


## Changelog

- conjectured: LOAD-3 as originally written said default rollover rolls positive, zeroes negative, and carries the negative as visible debt with no auto-true-up.
  refuted_by: Those are two different rules. Zeroing a negative and carrying it as debt cannot both hold. Under zero-based budgeting a forgiven overspend is not free, so the deficit must be booked somewhere or the ledger leaks and the holding tripwire loses meaning.
  learned: The YNAB resolution is correct here. Default zeroes the envelope by booking the deficit into next period available-to-allocate, surfaced as a visible line in the wizard. Standing per-envelope debt is the roll_all override only.
  criterion_now: ISC-25, ISC-26, ISC-28.

- conjectured: LOAD-1 every-dollar rule should hard-block save whenever income does not equal the sum of allocations.
  refuted_by: With expected-versus-actual income (NEW-2) a hard per-save block fights reality. Mid-month edits, late actuals, and partial wizard sessions would all be blocked, and viewing must never be blocked.
  learned: Enforce the every-dollar rule at wizard completion against expected income, not on every intermediate save. Divergence of actual from expected is shown as a dashboard delta and prompts re-allocation, never blocks viewing.
  criterion_now: ISC-30, ISC-31, ISC-32.

- conjectured: The project was scoped single-user and local-only, with a dormant unused `owner` column as the sole concession to a possible future multi-principal need.
  refuted_by: Nick now wants the option to onboard another person with only their email and their PocketSmith dev key. A dormant column does not deliver that; tenant scoping has to be threaded through every read and write, auth has to resolve a user, and each user's dev key has to be isolated secret material. Retrofitting that into an event-sourced single-principal ledger after the fact is the exact painful migration the architect review warned against.
  learned: Build multi-tenant from migration one and run single-tenant locally. Promote `owner` to a real `users` table with `user_id` on every tenant table, scope every store query by the authenticated user, put auth and secrets behind interfaces, and make deployment a one-switch config. Local-first is preserved as the default; cloud becomes additive. This is the cheapest path to "ready to put on the cloud if I decide".
  criterion_now: ISC-47, ISC-48, ISC-49, ISC-50, ISC-51, ISC-52, ISC-53, ISC-54, ISC-55, ISC-56, ISC-57, ISC-58, ISC-59.

- conjectured: The MVP UI explicitly excluded a PWA; mobile was Tailscale-reachable desktop web only.
  refuted_by: Nick requires the surface be optimised for both web app and installed PWA, on his phone. A PWA needs HTTPS for service-worker registration, which a localhost or self-signed local deploy does not cleanly provide.
  learned: Promote the PWA into MVP scope (manifest, service worker, installable, offline shell). Solve local HTTPS with tailscale serve on a *.ts.net hostname, which issues a real cert. Keep offline transaction capture and any mobile write queue out of scope so the PWA stays read-and-allocate, not a sync-conflict surface.
  criterion_now: ISC-60, ISC-61, ISC-62, ISC-63, ISC-64.

- conjectured: The every-dollar rule should be enforced at wizard completion (block save until allocations balance expected income), and actual-vs-expected income variance should show as a dashboard delta line.
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-26): zero-based applies to the budget (the plan), not to actual cash flow. A non-zero-based budget should raise an alert, never block. And income variance should not live on a dashboard line; income is itself an envelope per source, and actual differing from budgeted is a surplus/deficit on that income envelope.
  learned: Two separable rules. (1) The zero-based check compares total spending allocations to total budgeted income and raises an alert on imbalance; saving and viewing are never blocked. (2) Income envelopes are first-class, one per source, each holding a budgeted amount and an actual balance; the variance is the surplus/deficit. This keeps the envelope metaphor whole on the income side and removes the only hard wall the design still had.
  criterion_now: ISC-30, ISC-31, ISC-32, ISC-65.

- conjectured: Mid-month allocation editing is just editing an envelope's budgeted number in place.
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-26): a reallocation must say where the money comes from and whether the change is one-off or ongoing. Extra income parked in Savings could be a one-time move of this month's surplus, or a permanent decision to expect more income and save more every month. Editing a single number cannot express either the source or the recurrence.
  learned: A reallocation is a move with three parts: destination, named source, and scope (this-month-only vs ongoing). The named source keeps the plan zero-based by construction. There is a recurring budget template behind the months; an ongoing change edits the template (so apply-last-month carries it forward), a this-month change is a period override that leaves the template untouched. Skipping the source is allowed but raises the zero-based alert, consistent with alert-not-block.
  criterion_now: ISC-66, ISC-67, ISC-68, ISC-69.

- conjectured: FLAG-1 default rollover zeroes an overspent envelope and books the deficit into the next period's available-to-allocate pool; carrying a per-envelope negative is the roll_all exception.
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-26): any envelope in surplus or deficit should either be balanced by a move (either direction) or carry over, and the carryover must be shown explicitly per envelope. Silently zeroing an envelope and docking a pooled number hides the deficit; Nick wants it visible on the envelope it belongs to.
  learned: Carryover in both directions is the default, shown as an explicit starting-carryover figure on every envelope row. Surplus and deficit both roll unless the user moves money to balance them. drain_to_zero (use-it-or-lose-it) and annual_reset remain per-envelope overrides. Each envelope row shows four figures: starting carryover, this-period allocation, total available (carryover + allocation), and left (total - spent). The deficit is still conserved (it is the negative carryover, not a leak), so ISC-28 holds. The Edit Budget wizard, not an inline baseline edit, is where the recurring template is changed.
  criterion_now: ISC-25, ISC-26, ISC-70, ISC-71, ISC-72.

- conjectured: Envelopes are a flat list organised under text "group" labels (Bills, Lifestyle, Annual).
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-26): envelopes should be able to be parents of others, the totals should roll up, and a parent should also be summable with any allocation made directly to it. A text group label is just a header; it cannot hold money or roll up.
  learned: Envelopes form a tree via a self-referential parent_id. A parent is a first-class spendable, allocatable envelope. All four figures (carryover, allocation, total, left) roll up as sum-of-children plus any direct parent allocation/activity. The grid renders an expandable/collapsible hierarchy with rolled-up parent rows. The flat group string is subsumed by the tree (top-level envelopes act as groups).
  criterion_now: ISC-18, ISC-73, ISC-74, ISC-75.

- conjectured: LOAD-6 set the allocation ritual as a beginning-of-month wizard with apply-last-month as the default action.
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-26): the budget should just roll over to the next month; that is the entire purpose of a budget. No plan-a-month function is wanted. And budget changes should be viewable as a history.
  learned: The recurring budget template auto-applies to each new month with no manual step (ISC-29 refined from apply-last-month wizard to auto-roll; ISC-76 anti guards it). The zero-based check runs continuously and alerts rather than gating a wizard. Budget changes happen only through the Edit Budget wizard or move-money (ongoing scope), and every change — including the automatic monthly rolls — is recorded in a viewable budget history (ISC-77). This supersedes the LOAD-6 allocation-ritual decision.
  criterion_now: ISC-29, ISC-76, ISC-77.

- conjectured: The parallel build's UI placeholder seams (income shown as demo data, Reconcile fully placeholder) were an acceptable interim, and the engine-level ISC verification (71/77) meant the app was substantially done.
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-26), using it live: the budget read "balanced" with three income categories and no envelopes, because the income section was hardcoded placeholder never wired to the API while the real backend had zero envelopes. Engine unit tests passed but the user-facing surfaces were not real. The placeholders were actively misleading.
  learned: Remove all placeholder/demo data; a screen with no data shows an explicit empty state, never invented numbers. Seed the real budget from PocketSmith: the category tree (105 categories) becomes the envelope tree with parent/child preserved; income vs expense is read from the budget endpoint's income/expense block; budgeted amounts come from the budget forecast; starting carryover comes from the rollover baseline doc (balances + start dates Nick captured); the 6038 loaded transactions map to envelopes by category. Add a real create-envelope flow with an income/expense type toggle. The empty/zero-based status must distinguish "not set up" from "balanced". Verification must drive the real browser, not just engine tests.
  criterion_now: ISC-83, ISC-84, ISC-85, ISC-86, ISC-87, ISC-88, ISC-31, ISC-65.

- conjectured: There are two envelope kinds (income, expense), income variance is actual-minus-budgeted, and budgets are monthly; importing PocketSmith forecasts as monthly allocations is sufficient.
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-27) plus the reconciliation session fbf081be. (a) There are THREE types: income, expense, and saving (saving is an expense envelope with a different label) — this is why Emergency Fund imported as an absurd $20k/month budget; it is a saving envelope holding a balance, not a monthly allocation. (b) Income works as capital: each income envelope has a target and a specified portion that flows to a shared expense-capital pool; expense and saving envelopes draw from that pool, and the income surplus/deficit is capital-provided minus total allocations (over/under BUDGETING, not actual-vs-target). (c) Expense budgets are weekly, monthly, or annual, not all monthly. (d) The reconciliation proved PocketSmith does not roll child spend up to a parent budget (Recreation shows +$2,660 left while ~$7,350 over across its unbudgeted children) — envelope-os must roll child spend up to the nearest budgeted ancestor. PocketSmith's post-2026-02-28 reorg also makes its displayed rollover balances approximate, so imported carryover is a baseline, not a ledger truth.
  learned: Model envelopes with an explicit type (income/expense/saving). Income = target + capital-to-expense; expense/saving draw from pooled capital; surplus/deficit = capital minus allocations. Budgets carry a period (weekly/monthly/annual) normalised to monthly. Roll child spend up to the budgeted ancestor. Re-import savings-group categories as saving envelopes (balance, not monthly budget), and treat annual/weekly PocketSmith budgets by their real period.
  criterion_now: ISC-91, ISC-92, ISC-93, ISC-94, ISC-95.

- conjectured: Income works as a two-step capital model — each income envelope has a target and a specified portion routed into a shared expense-capital pool, and expense/saving/envelope budgets draw from that pool.
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-27): the capital-allocation step is unnecessary complexity. His spreadsheet's Full Budget sheet simply balances total income against total expenses; there is no sub-pool. Scrap capitalToExpense.
  learned: The income budget itself is the balancing figure. Zero-base = sum(income budgets) minus sum(expense + saving + envelope budgets); residual is the surplus/deficit. Remove the capitalToExpenseCents field from the model, API, seeder, and UI. The income "received minus budget" variance view is retained.
  criterion_now: ISC-92, ISC-93.

- conjectured: BudgetBuddy's bank feeds and categorisation come from PocketSmith; each tenant supplies their own PocketSmith developer key (ISC-3), the client is generated from the pinned PocketSmith OpenAPI spec (ISC-1, ISC-2), and categories source from the PocketSmith category tree (ISC-84, ISC-86).
  refuted_by: Nick (2026-06-29): PocketSmith is a paid dependency and, critically, a per-user external account. A product cannot require every user to hold their own PocketSmith subscription and key. BudgetBuddy is already multi-tenant (Store scoped by userId, ISC-47/48), so PocketSmith was the sole structural blocker to serving other people; it is "essentially just a bridge".
  learned: Replace PocketSmith with direct Australian bank feeds via Basiq (CDR) behind a new `IngestProvider` port, so the provider is swappable and the envelope/budget/store/UI layers stay untouched (Tx.categoryId is the only coupling point). The app holds one Basiq API key; users link banks via the in-app CDR Consent UI with no key to obtain and no credential reaching BudgetBuddy. BudgetBuddy now owns categorisation (Basiq enrich plus user-editable effective-dated rules) because inherited per-user categorisation is unavailable; the existing category-to-envelope mapping and Inbox fallback are preserved by synthesising a stable internal categoryId. Build single-user now, product-shaped; defer multi-user CDR-representative onboarding.
  criterion_now: ISC-105, ISC-106, ISC-107, ISC-108, ISC-109, ISC-110, ISC-111, ISC-112, ISC-113, ISC-114, ISC-115, ISC-116, ISC-117, ISC-118, ISC-119, ISC-120, ISC-121, ISC-122, ISC-123, ISC-124, ISC-125, ISC-126, ISC-127, ISC-128.

- conjectured: completePrevious closing only OPEN versions at the day before effectiveFrom fully implements "replace my budget from this date" (v1.4.x budget chronology design).
  refuted_by: Reproduction 2026-08-10 — after a mid-month version boundary (which closes the baseline as a COMPLETED version ending inside the month), a later ordinary save left the completed window contributing its early-month days; overlap-sums then blended old and new amounts (typed 70000, stored 76452). Independently confirmed by 5-Whys analysis: supersession was keyed on line STATE (open/completed) instead of window OVERLAP with [effectiveFrom, ∞).
  learned: The invariant is "no prior version of this envelope contributes on or after effectiveFrom". Supersede by window overlap: truncate every version whose effectiveTo is null OR >= effectiveFrom; fully-superseded lines become degenerate windows that resolve to zero contribution.
  criterion_now: ISC-137, ISC-138, ISC-139.

- conjectured: the two budget-versions-overlap test failures (409 on the from-the-1st save) were a supersession regression introduced sometime after 2026-08-14.
  refuted_by: 2026-09-02 investigation — the tests' MID version starts on the 5th; planVersionWrite classifies a version starting after TODAY as scheduled, so on days 1-4 of any month the from-the-1st save correctly 409s (scheduled_conflict, doctrine D3) awaiting consent. Green on 10-14 Aug, red on 2 Sep, engine unchanged. The wizard UI already handles it ("Replace scheduled budgets and save" → supersedeScheduled) and the refusal contract is deterministically covered in budget-window-integrity.test.ts.
  learned: a test whose fixture dates are derived from the CURRENT month can encode a hidden day-of-month dependency; any "pre-existing failure" claim should note the calendar before assuming regression. Fixed by mirroring the UI's consented retry in the tests, keeping the exact-amount guard deterministic year-round.
  criterion_now: ISC-297.

- conjectured: ensureMonth's materialise-if-empty guard ("an already-populated month is left untouched so edits are never clobbered") plus rederive-on-edit fully keeps materialised months true to the budget chronology.
  refuted_by: 2026-09 live ledger — months were materialised through 2030-12 by a long-range view; envelopes created 12-13 Aug (Compliance, Leasing Fees) existed in the chronology but reached only their creation month, leaving every later month's plan short by exactly $141.67 with no surface explaining why. Creation was the one line-writing path with no rederive, and the skip guard blocked the lazy repair too.
  learned: "Never clobber" and "never skip" are separable: existing rows are the edits to protect; MISSING rows are always safe to add because a deliberate zero is stored as a row. ensureMonth now reconciles missing envelopes on every call, and creation invokes the same rederive as edits — materialised months converge on the chronology from both directions.
  criterion_now: ISC-291, ISC-292, ISC-293, ISC-294, ISC-295, ISC-296.

